   "line": [
    "Be7"
   ],
   "spoken": "A neat move-order trick — sometimes lead with the bishop to e7. It quietly prevents an early pin, and if the opponent develops the knight, the reply keeps you out of the more annoying lines of the opening. Small orderings can dodge a lot of theory.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 45.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk1nr/ppp1bppp/4p3/3P4/3P4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "cxd5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 49,
   "fen": "rnbqk1nr/ppp1bppp/8/3p4/3P4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "exd5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 53,
   "fen": "rnbqk1nr/ppp1bppp/8/3p4/3P4/2N2N2/PP2PPPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 1 5",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 82.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk1nr/pp2bppp/2p5/3p4/3P4/2N2N2/PP2PPPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The cool treatment is a pawn to c6 and then repositioning the bishop from e7 across to d6. It looks like a waste of tempi, but it's the setup strong players favour — offering to trade the dark-squared bishops, which helps the defender, since that bishop is often awkward with no good parking spot.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 108.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk1nr/pp3ppp/2pb4/3p4/3P1B2/2N2N2/PP2PPPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 2 7",
   "line": [
    "Bf4",
    "Bd6"
   ],
   "spoken": "You might object that no pieces are being developed — how can that be safe? It's all down to the state of the centre. With a solid pawn chain and a closed middle, the opponent can't break with the central push while their own king is stuck there, so you can afford several moves with one piece. Don't be a slave to the rule — judge the position intuitively.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 152.5,
   "fen": "rnb1k1nr/pp3ppp/2pq4/3p4/3P4/2N2N2/PP2PPPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "Bxd6",
    "Qxd6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 160,
   "fen": "rnb1k1nr/pp3ppp/2pq4/3p4/3P4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 176.5,
   "fen": "rn2k1nr/pp3ppp/2pq4/3p1b2/3P4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Bf5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 181,
   "fen": "rnb1k1nr/pp3ppp/2pq4/3p4/3P4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "The bishop develops to f5 before the knights, and for a concrete reason: allow the opponent's bishop to reach d3 and this active post is lost forever. The alternative pin square is a viable developing move but tends to be overrated here.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 200,
   "fen": "rn2k1nr/pp3ppp/2pq4/3p1b2/3P4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R w KQkq - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Bf5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 241.5,
   "fen": "rn2k1nr/pp3ppp/2pq4/3p1b2/3P4/2N1PN2/PP2BPPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 2 9",
   "line": [
    "Be2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 259,
   "fen": "r3k1nr/pp1n1ppp/2pq4/3p1b2/3P4/2N1PN2/PP2BPPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 3 10",
   "line": [
    "Nd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "A subtlety in the knight order — develop to d7 before f6, because leading with the other knight would invite a central leap into e5. Deny that outpost first, then bring the second knight out. And if the queen probes the b-pawn, defend with a rook: yes, the rook is tied to defence, but the queen is equally tied to the attack — a happy trade of duties.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 291,
   "fen": "r3k1nr/pp1n1ppp/2pq4/3p1b2/3P4/2N1PN2/PP2BPPP/R2Q1RK1 b kq - 4 10",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
